{
 "turns_text": [
  "[SEG0001] person_1 (DOCTOR): Sorry, one minute, the screen has frozen on the last patient. Mrs Kamala, what brings you in today?",
  "[SEG0002] person_2 (PATIENT): I am alright only. She dragged me here for no reason. I am tired, please be quick.",
  "[SEG0003] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Doctor, she went down twice this week. She will say she did not fall, only slid.",
  "[SEG0004] person_1 (DOCTOR): Slid down? Say it once more, I was on the wrong tab.",
  "[SEG0005] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Twice. Once she slid off the chair, once near the bathroom. Both times after she stood up.",
  "[SEG0006] person_2 (PATIENT): It was not a fall. The mat is slippery and I sat down slowly. Don't make a drama.",
  "[SEG0007] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Amma, you were giddy the second you stood—",
  "[SEG0008] person_2 (PATIENT): A little giddy, that is all. No need to shout, I am already tired.",
  "[SEG0009] person_1 (DOCTOR): Does that giddiness come when you stand up from sitting?",
  "[SEG0010] person_2 (PATIENT): The tablets are too many. Morning I swallow three, and one sleeping tablet at night. I am fed up of all this.",
  "[SEG0011] person_1 (DOCTOR): Madam, I asked about the giddiness, we will come to the tablets. When you stand, is it there?",
  "[SEG0012] person_2 (PATIENT): Yes. When I get up it comes, and if I hold the wall it settles.",
  "[SEG0013] person_1 (DOCTOR): Did you injure yourself, your head hit or anything, or—",
  "[SEG0014] person_2 (PATIENT): No. I didn't bang anything. I only slid.",
  "[SEG0015] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Monday was the first one, and the bathroom one was day before yesterday. She told me only at night. I saw no bruise.",
  "[SEG0016] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Her legs are weak, doctor. She told me, and now she will not admit it.",
  "[SEG0017] person_2 (PATIENT): My legs are not weak. Don't invent things.",
  "[SEG0018] person_1 (DOCTOR): Let me open what the nurse entered... wrong Kamala, one second. Lying blood pressure was one hundred fifty-two over eighty-eight, pulse seventy-four. Standing it was one twenty-four over seventy-six, pulse ninety-two.",
  "[SEG0019] person_2 (PATIENT): That cuff is always tight. It frightens everyone for nothing.",
  "[SEG0020] person_1 (DOCTOR): Kindly repeat the BP tablets, the list on this screen is half blank.",
  "[SEG0021] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Amlodipine five milligram in the morning, telmisartan forty milligram in the morning, atenolol twenty-five milligram, and alprazolam zero point two five at night for sleep.",
  "[SEG0022] person_2 (PATIENT): Atenolol I take only sometimes. Not every day, like she thinks.",
  "[SEG0023] person_1 (DOCTOR): The old slip says atenolol twenty-five every morning. So daily or not?",
  "[SEG0024] person_2 (PATIENT): Most mornings. If the pulse feels very slow I leave it off.",
  "[SEG0025] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Two or three times a week she skips it. I counted the strip myself.",
  "[SEG0026] person_1 (DOCTOR): And the alprazolam, every night?",
  "[SEG0027] person_2 (PATIENT): Yes. Otherwise I don't sleep at all. Since my husband... anyway, leave that.",
  "[SEG0028] person_1 (DOCTOR): How long has this giddiness been going on?",
  "[SEG0029] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Two weeks, at least. She will shrink it so we can go home.",
  "[SEG0030] person_2 (PATIENT): After the pooja, about ten days. Not two weeks. I am not shrinking anything.",
  "[SEG0031] person_1 (DOCTOR): Any chest pain or shortness of breath with it?",
  "[SEG0032] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Amma, listen to him—",
  "[SEG0033] person_2 (PATIENT): No chest pain and no breathing trouble. The torn mat is a separate thing, I have said it before.",
  "[SEG0034] person_1 (DOCTOR): Any palpitations, heart racing?",
  "[SEG0035] person_2 (PATIENT): What?",
  "[SEG0036] person_1 (DOCTOR): Thumping in the chest, the heart going fast.",
  "[SEG0037] person_2 (PATIENT): No, nothing like that. Only the giddy feeling.",
  "[SEG0038] person_3 (CAREGIVER): She has diabetes also, metformin five hundred morning and night. Last week she said the sugar was one forty something.",
  "[SEG0039] person_1 (DOCTOR): Diabetes, yes, it is on the old note. When you slid, did you black out, lose consciousness?",
  "[SEG0040] person_2 (PATIENT): No. I was awake the whole time. How many times, I only slid.",
  "[SEG0041] person_1 (DOCTOR): Anything wrong with the ears? I thought the room was spinning.",
  "[SEG0042] person_2 (PATIENT): I never said spinning. My ears are perfectly fine. She adds stories.",
  "[SEG0043] person_3 (CAREGIVER): Sorry, doctor, that was my mistake. She only said giddy, not that the room moved.",
  "[SEG0044] person_1 (DOCTOR): I am stopping the atenolol from today. The standing pressure has fallen, and that tablet can make you slide like this. Continue amlodipine five and telmisartan forty the same way.",
  "[SEG0045] person_2 (PATIENT): Stop it fully? Then my pressure will shoot up, no? I am already worried.",
  "[SEG0046] person_1 (DOCTOR): Only the atenolol stops. Keep the other two. If the giddiness is still there after one week, come back. If you fall and hit your head, come sooner, don't wait at home.",
  "[SEG0047] person_3 (CAREGIVER): I can bring whatever is left in the strips.",
  "[SEG0048] person_1 (DOCTOR): Yes, bring the strips next time. And get up slowly, sit on the edge of the bed a full minute before standing. There is a message pinging— no, we are done, you may go."
 ],
